    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi.jpg" alt="Muhammad al-Idrisi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">Muhammad al-Idrisi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estatua_de_Al-Idrisi_bajo_el_baluarte_de_los_Mallorquines,_Ceuta_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mario Sánchez Bueno; derivative work by Ecemaml, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Reval, the medieval name of Tallinn, grew on a site long used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>, who had a fort on the limestone hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toompea">Toompea</a> and a harbour that the Arab geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a> probably refers to in 1154. In 1219 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II of Denmark</a> landed with a crusading army, defeated the Estonians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lindanise">Battle of Lindanise</a>, the battle at which, according to later legend, the Danish flag, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flag-of-denmark">Dannebrog</a>, fell from heaven, and built a castle on Toompea. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> lost the area to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> in 1227 but regained it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stensby">Treaty of Stensby</a> in 1238, and their Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> covered the northern provinces of Harria and Vironia. German merchants, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, settled below the castle, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friars</a> founded the convent of St Catherine in the 1240s. In 1248 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-iv-of-denmark">Eric IV of Denmark</a> granted the town Lübeck law, giving its citizens self-government, their own council and the right to seek legal guidance from Lübeck; the history of Reval as a self-governing city is dated from this grant. Over the following decades Reval walled its lower town, obtained further privileges from the Danish kings and joined the network of German trading towns that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lubeck"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lubeck.jpg" alt="Lübeck" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%BCbeck_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oleg Dejan, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Reval lived from long-distance trade. Its merchants bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, wax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> and hides from Novgorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, often through the Hanseatic counting house at Novgorod, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterhof-novgorod">Peterhof</a>, and shipped them west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, while salt, cloth, herring, wine and metals moved east. Grain from the estates of Harria and Vironia was another export, and the town also traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>. Its population was small, probably several thousand people at most, and sharply divided. The wealthiest German merchants belonged to the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a>, whose guildhall was built in the early fifteenth century, and only its members could sit on the council. Unmarried merchants and merchants' assistants formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brotherhood-of-blackheads">Brotherhood of the Blackheads</a>, named after its patron Saint Maurice, which also helped defend the city. Craftsmen organised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canute-guild">Canute Guild</a> and the more mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-guild">Olaf Guild</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>, called <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/undeutsche">Undeutsche</a></em> (non-Germans) in the sources, worked as porters, carters, servants and labourers and were largely excluded from the privileged trades and from high office. Religious life centred on the parish churches of St Olaf and St Nicholas, the Dominican convent and the Cistercian nunnery of St Michael, and the guilds and wealthy families endowed altars, chapels, almshouses and other charitable foundations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livonian-war.jpg" alt="Livonian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siege_of_Narva_1558.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Boris Chorikov, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As a merchant town Reval relied mainly on its walls and its citizens rather than on an army of its own. The ring of walls around the lower town was extended and strengthened from the thirteenth to the sixteenth century, eventually reaching about 2.4 kilometres with more than forty towers, around half of which still stand. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> tower known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiek-in-de-kok">Kiek in de Kök</a>, built from the 1470s, and the massive artillery tower called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fat-margaret">Fat Margaret</a> at the harbour gate, completed around 1530, show the town's adaptation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>. Citizens served in the defence by quarters, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brotherhood-of-blackheads">Brotherhood of the Blackheads</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> supplied men and equipment. The castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toompea">Toompea</a>, rebuilt as a convent castle by the Livonian Order after 1346, housed the ruler's garrison rather than the town's. Reval's fortifications proved their worth in 1343 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georges-night-uprising">St George's Night Uprising</a>, when Estonian rebels besieged the town until the Livonian Order's knights crushed them. The town contributed money and ships to the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, including the campaigns against Muscovy led by Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolter-von-plettenberg">Wolter von Plettenberg</a> in the early sixteenth century. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> Russian armies came close, and after Reval passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> its walls withstood the long Russian sieges of 1570–1571 and 1577.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tartu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tartu.jpg" alt="Tartu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tartu_(2019).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Boreaallane, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Reval was not a sovereign state but a self-governing town under an overlord: the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> until 1346, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and its Livonian branch. Within its walls, however, the lower town ran its own affairs under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law. The council, a small body of councillors headed by burgomasters, co-opted its members for life from the merchant elite of the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a>, issued ordinances, administered justice, regulated markets and crafts, and conducted relations with other towns. In difficult cases it sought rulings from the council of Lübeck, the mother city of its law. The town also sent delegates to the Livonian diets, where the Order, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> nobility and the towns met, and to Hanseatic assemblies, and Reval, Riga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a> formed the leading Livonian group within the Hanse. A sharp legal divide separated the lower town from the upper town on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toompea">Toompea</a>, the seat of the ruler's officials, the bishop of Reval and the knighthood of Harria and Vironia, which had its own jurisdiction, and relations between the two were often tense. The council used its wealth to secure commercial privileges, and together with the other Livonian towns it barred foreign merchants from trading directly with Russians or with each other, so that the Russian trade had to pass through the hands of Reval's citizens.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/st-olafs-church-tallinn"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-olafs-church-tallinn.jpg" alt="St. Olaf's Church, Tallinn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/st-olafs-church-tallinn">St. Olaf's Church, Tallinn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Olaf%27s_church,_Tallinn,_July_2008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Olga Itenberg, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Reval's most enduring legacy is physical. Because the town escaped large-scale destruction for centuries, its lower town keeps much of its medieval street plan, walls, towers, merchant houses, guildhalls and churches, and the Historic Centre (Old Town) of Tallinn was inscribed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage List</a> in 1997. Its Gothic town hall, completed in 1404, is often described as the oldest surviving town hall in the Baltic region and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, and the spire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-olafs-church-tallinn">St Olaf's Church</a>, rebuilt several times, probably made it one of the tallest buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the sixteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> artist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernt-notke">Bernt Notke</a> painted the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danse-macabre-notke">Danse Macabre</a></em> now displayed in St Nicholas' Church and the altarpiece of the Church of the Holy Spirit, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermen-rode">Hermen Rode</a>'s high altar survives in St Nicholas. Reval also served as a vital link in the Hanseatic network, carrying the Russian trade between Novgorod and the West and helping to draw north-eastern Europe into the commercial economy of the Baltic. Its legal and social institutions outlasted its medieval status: Lübeck law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and the German-speaking urban elite remained dominant in the city's civic life until the early twentieth century, and in 1918 the city became, as Tallinn, the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Valdemar IV of Denmark" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waldemar_IV_Otherday_of_Denmark_c_1375_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown contemporary 14th-century artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Reval's position changed long before its self-governing life ended. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georges-night-uprising">St George's Night Uprising</a> of 1343 persuaded the Danish crown that its Estonian lands were too costly to hold, and in 1346 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV</a> sold them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, which entrusted them to its Livonian branch. Reval kept its privileges but now lay inside the Livonian Confederation, a loose union of the Order, the bishoprics and the towns. The closure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterhof-novgorod">Hanseatic Peterhof</a> at Novgorod by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III of Moscow</a> in 1494 disrupted trade, though Reval and the other Livonian towns continued to profit as intermediaries for Western merchants dealing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Lutheran preaching spread in the early 1520s, and in 1524 iconoclastic riots stripped many church interiors; the council took control of church affairs, weakening the Catholic Order's hold. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> in 1558, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> exposed the Confederation's weakness. Russia took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a>, diverting Russian trade to Narva, and the Order could not protect its subjects. In June 1561 the city and the knighthood of Harria and Vironia swore allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-xiv-of-sweden">Eric XIV of Sweden</a>, who promised to confirm their privileges. Reval became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-estonia">Swedish Duchy of Estonia</a>, ending its life as a self-governing Hanseatic town of Livonia.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Reval, the medieval Hanseatic town of Tallinn, governed itself under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law for more than three centuries and grew rich carrying Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and wax to the West, leaving one of the best-preserved medieval old towns in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> before it submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1561.</em></p>

